    "content": "The World Trade Organization (WTO) is the only global international organization managing rules of trade between nations, aiming to ensure trade flows smoothly, predictably, and freely. Established in 1995, it serves 166 members, accounting for 98% of world trade, by negotiating agreements, settling disputes, and monitoring trade policies. Purpose and FunctionsThe WTO serves as a forum for trade negotiations and dispute settlement, with the goal of raising living standards, creating jobs, and improving life quality. Key functions include: Trade Negotiation: Setting rules for international commerce in goods, services, and intellectual property. Dispute Settlement: Providing a system to resolve trade conflicts between member countries. Monitoring: Reviewing national trade policies and ensuring transparency. Capacity Building: Assisting developing economies to participate in the global trading system. Rules and PrinciplesWTO rules are based on agreements signed by member countries and ratified in their parliaments. Core principles include: Non-Discrimination: Comprising \"most-favoured-nation\" (treating all members equally) and \"national treatment\" (treating foreigners and locals equally). Fair Competition: Promoting open, fair competition. Transparency: Requiring members to disclose trade regulations. Exceptions: Allowing flexibility for environmental protection, national security, and developing country needs. How the WTO Works Structure: Decisions are made by member governments through consensus, usually via the Ministerial Conference (every two years) or the General Council in Geneva. Decision-Making: Member-driven, with all decisions adopted by consensus of all members. Secretariat: Located in Geneva, Switzerland, it provides administrative support and is currently led by Director-General Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala. Key ChallengesThe WTO currently faces pressure to reform, with critics citing outdated rules in a rapidly changing world and a weakened dispute settlement system due to lack of consensus among members.",
